okf:
  version: "1.0"
  kind: engineering-journal
  task:
    id: windows-tui-first-character-draft-guard-20260828
    title: Preserve the first manually typed TUI character during external wake injection
    status: verified

  reported_observation:
    platform: Windows 11
    symptom: The first character typed into the routed TUI repeatedly disappears
    affected_existing_session: true
    exact_raw_input_capture_available: false

  installed_runtime_evidence:
    version: 0.2.36
    running_entrypoint: C:/Users/djlov/.local/share/ciel-runtime/ciel_runtime.py
    installed_windows_conpty_blob: 0a6fc958ef7945f2de82137669475e85b1c295af
    matching_repository_revision: 8785786
    latest_worktree_windows_conpty_blob: 72bdaa02424e3436f66cf0dabc480bba1f9dc220
    conclusion: >-
      The running installation predates commit 83a03be. Its new startup terminal
      reset therefore cannot be the cause of the observed running-session symptom.

  direct_reproduction:
    fresh_claude_tui:
      claude_version: 2.1.250
      installed_runtime_result: CLAUDE_FIRST_INPUT_OK
      worktree_runtime_result: CLAUDE_FIRST_INPUT_OK
    post_local_command_transition:
      command: /help then Escape
      result: CLAUDE_FIRST_INPUT_OK
    native_parent_console_transport:
      method: >-
        Write X, Y, and Z as Win32 KEY_EVENT records into the parent console,
        let the production ReadConsoleW pump forward them to a raw ConPTY child,
        and require the child to exit zero only on the exact XYZ bytes.
      observed_parent_read: "b'XYZ'"
      child_exit: 0
    conclusion: >-
      A fresh TUI, a local command state transition, and the base parent-console
      transport did not reproduce a dropped first byte.

  confirmed_code_hazard:
    external_wake_clear_sequence: Ctrl+U
    previous_guard_for_manual_draft: false
    behavior: >-
      ChannelPromptInjector could write Ctrl+U plus an external wake prompt while
      an operator draft was already present. That operation deterministically
      clears the draft, including its first character.
    causal_link_to_reported_existing_session: unconfirmed
    reason: >-
      The current durable channel cursor was at the latest message and wake claims
      were empty when inspected, so no historical log proves that a wake collision
      occurred at the exact reported keystroke.

  implementation:
    parent_input_tracking:
      source: ciel_runtime_support/windows_conpty.py
      states:
        - unsubmitted printable text
        - VT escape sequence parsing
        - bracketed-paste boundaries
        - submit and clear controls
      public_signal: manual_input_active
    injection_guard:
      source: ciel_runtime_support/channel_injection.py
      behavior: >-
        Return false before writing Ctrl+U, prompt bytes, or Enter whenever the
        ConPTY reports an unsubmitted manual draft. Existing pending-message
        rollback and polling then defer delivery without destroying user input.

  verification:
    channel_injection_tests: "14 passed"
    windows_conpty_tests: "28 passed, including native Windows ConPTY"
    ruff_changed_files: passed
    full_npm_test:
      unit: "1345 passed, 44 skipped"
      router: "1056 passed"
      channel: "397 passed, 80 skipped"
      runtime: "258 passed, 12 skipped"
      total: "3056 passed, 136 skipped, 0 failed"
    native_draft_guard_probe:
      parent_read: "b'XYZ'"
      manual_draft_active: true
      external_submission: false
      log: channel_input_injection_deferred reason=parent_input_draft
      child_exit: 0
    claude_post_command_probe: CLAUDE_FIRST_INPUT_OK

  limitations:
    - >-
      The original existing-session keystroke stream was not recorded, so this
      journal does not claim that external Ctrl+U injection was the proven cause.
    - >-
      The guard applies to the native Windows ConPTY transport, which was confirmed
      in the running process tree. The legacy console-input fallback has no parent
      read pump from which to observe a manual draft.
